    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d471">

                <pb facs="0700" n="598" xml:id="pg598"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">765.84/141</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Italy</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Kirk</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 911</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Rome</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-01-18">January 18,
                        1935</date>.<lb/>[Received January 30.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>:</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p>In conclusion, I have the honor to report that there are indications that the
                    British Ambassador here has been very active in endeavoring to effect a
                    compromise between the Italian and Abyssinian points of view. In British circles
                    here it is felt that despite the Abyssinian request for a discussion of the
                    Walwal incident during the present session of the League of Nations Council, a
                    settlement of the question will be reached before such discussion takes place.
                    It appears that of the three demands made by the Italians (namely, that an
                    apology on the part of Abyssinia be published in an official communiqué, that a
                    cash indemnity be deposited in a bank in Eritrea to be distributed among the
                    sufferers of the December 5th incident, and that the Abyssinians salute the
                    Italian flag), the first two are more or less acceptable to the Abyssinians, so
                    that at the moment the efforts of the British are being directed toward a
                    settlement of the last question.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Alexander Kirk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
